  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nToday went more or less as expected, with storms again focused\nover the Gila Region and down into the Bootheel. Perhaps a little\nmore activity into Sierra and Luna Counties than I would have\nexpected.\n\nOur upper tropospheric low is crawling towards Coahuila, and again\ninfluenced convective development in \"near-west\" Texas. Some of\non-going convection has drifted into Hudspeth County, roughly\nassociated with an upper level deformation zone associated with\nthe low. Expect we'll see off and on convective showers in parts\nof Hudspeth much of the night, but with no real impact.\n\nOverall expect a continued uptick in convection coverage tomorrow,\nmainly near and east of the Rio Grande, under the influence of\nthat upper low. Its track is slowing down, however, and now Sunday\nlooks to be a little more generous for precip chances area-wide,\nrather than shifting west. The western preference now slips to\nMonday with the slower track. \n\nThen the ridge builds west, limiting convection coverage Tue and\nWednesday, and boosting temps back towards the 100 mark. But\nmodels are continuing to suggest this will be short lived, with\nthe eastern edge of the ridge breaking down. This will allow the\nweak trailing end of shortwave troughs to skirt the area, and\ncombined with typical mid/late-monsoon recycled moisture, should \nbe enough to boost precip chances again later in the week.",
